      "copiedBoundaries": [
        {
          "before": "The momentum in this notation is $\\vec{p}=m\\vec{v}$.\nIn classical mechanics, m is treated as independent of speed.\nWe keep the source's notation below, but its high-speed m denotes frame-dependent \"relativistic mass.\"",
          "after": "Near light speed, the momentum-rate law (8.8) keeps its form.\nAfter two and a half centuries of the constant-mass picture dating from Newton, the source introduces velocity-dependent relativistic mass."
        },
        {
          "before": "Figure 227 shows how the source's relativistic mass depends on speed.\nThe mass variable in the curve is m, not a rest mass that increases with speed.",
          "after": "At speeds much smaller than light speed, $\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}$ differs very little from $1$.\nRelativistic mass is then almost equal to rest mass."
        },
        {
          "before": "For its rocket example at $v=$ $10$ km/s, the source gives:\n$\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}=0.99999999944$",
          "after": "In this low-speed example, the relativistic-mass increase is too small to detect in the source's account.\nCharged-particle accelerators, however, can bring particles close to light speed."
        },
        {
          "before": "The qualitative point is that Newtonian mechanics alone cannot describe high-speed particle trajectories.\nCurved tracks of charged particles in a magnetic field provide observations for testing relativistic momentum.",
          "after": "Using relation $(8.9)$ in the source's momentum notation gives:"
        },
        {
          "before": "For a finite force acting along the motion, acceleration tends to $0$, and the increase in speed becomes ever smaller.\nA body with rest mass can approach light speed but cannot reach it in finite time through this continued acceleration.\nThis does not mean that rest mass increases, nor that relativistic acceleration is always force divided by m.",
          "after": "Designing charged-particle accelerators requires relativistic equations of motion, making relativity part of engineering.\nA cyclotron is useful for accelerating charged particles, but high-speed acceleration and curved paths cannot be estimated while ignoring relativistic effects."
        }
      ],
